            <ArticleTitle>Chronic pain after methysergide: a new cause of ischemic monomelic neuropathy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>337-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Chronic foot pain developed in a 43-year-old woman after taking methysergide, a potent vasoconstrictor. The etiology was believed to be ischemic monomelic neuropathy, which results from the compromise of blood flow to an extremity. The patient eventually required a surgical sympathectomy after lumbar sympathetic blocks failed to provide permanent relief.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
